WEBVTT

1
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:57.640
Andrey Venuto: Hey guys, Kevin… I was in a meeting with Kevin, he's joining soon.

2
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:15.669
Andrey Venuto: So, for the OpenZeppelin folks, I will reschedule a meeting for randomness, okay? I had to cancel yesterday's one, but…

3
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.809
Andrey Venuto: Try to buy something next week.

4
00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:42.839
Joseph Denman: Who is the wolf?

5
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:52.670
Andrey Venuto: We're the seniors. Oh, yeah.

6
00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.390
Andrey Venuto: new people.

7
00:04:27.270 --> 00:04:30.329
Andrey Venuto: Yeah, I just pinged Kevin. Let's wait a minute more.

8
00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:12.550
Andrey Venuto: Okay.

9
00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.219
Andrey Venuto: He's almost here, guys.

10
00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:42.820
Kevin Millikin: Hey, sorry I'm… sorry I'm late. I'm in the wrong time zone still, so I was going to be here in an hour.

11
00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.060
Kevin Millikin: Let me see if I can get the… Agenda up real quick.

12
00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.110
Kevin Millikin: Okay, I think the agenda's on screen now.

13
00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:28.640
Andrey Venuto: Yes.

14
00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:29.230
Kevin Millikin: Thank you.

15
00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:37.719
Kevin Millikin: Excellent. Everyone's here. So, December 3rd, the first thing, this is something I put on there,

16
00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:48.820
Kevin Millikin: some people were using the language, and they felt like the compact equality between opaque U and 8 arrays was not what they expected.

17
00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:56.550
Kevin Millikin: So it's reference equality that they're actually the same object, in the JS implementation. And that's…

18
00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:05.060
Kevin Millikin: Working as intended, from the standpoint that that's what we intentionally implemented, and that's what it says in the language reference, but it doesn't seem very useful.

19
00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:15.949
Kevin Millikin: So, my first… my first inclination was to say that's all we can do, because it's an opaque type, but we do actually say that we know what's represented as a UN8 array.

20
00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:23.230
Kevin Millikin: And so we can define it, and its definition is some JavaScript code.

21
00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:30.489
Kevin Millikin: Strict equality. So we could actually say that it's something else, that it's… that it's pairwise, strict equality.

22
00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:33.750
Kevin Millikin: Element-wise, stretching quality. Kent.

23
00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.719
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, that's fine. What about strings?

24
00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:49.079
Kevin Millikin: Strings are already, strict equality is already, e- equal values.

25
00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:56.940
Kevin Millikin: Not references quality, I believe. I'm no longer a JavaScript expert, but I think that's what it is.

26
00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.560
Kevin Millikin: Thomas?

27
00:07:58.560 --> 00:07:59.220
Kent Dybvig: Okay.

28
00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:06.150
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, I just wanted to highlight, like, in… in circuit, this is defined through hash equality.

29
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:12.529
Thomas Kerber: So, having a defined… As value equality makes sense there as well.

30
00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:15.239
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, that's… that's a great point. I was…

31
00:08:15.350 --> 00:08:31.880
Kevin Millikin: I was, looking at the representation circuit, and I… and I… and it troubled me a little bit that it seemed like there was a mismatch there, that you would have something that was… that was not equal, in the JS implementation, and that was equal in circuit, and that was a potential soundness problem.

32
00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.200
Kevin Millikin: Okay, so I think we'll agree to make this change.

33
00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:41.259
Kevin Millikin: And I think it was Iskander that pointed it out, so thanks for… thanks for bringing that to our attention.

34
00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.230
Joseph Denman: One more… Comment on this?

35
00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:47.300
Kevin Millikin: Yeah.

36
00:08:48.510 --> 00:08:56.340
Joseph Denman: It would… so, are the only, admissible, parameters for the opaque type… you went 8 array and string, are there…

37
00:08:57.260 --> 00:08:58.250
Kevin Millikin: This time, yes.

38
00:08:58.590 --> 00:09:03.850
Kevin Millikin: Okay. We have… we have reserved the right to add any that we might… that we might want to add.

39
00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:13.510
Kevin Millikin: And we've even discussed the idea of using opaque types for other kinds of types than JavaScript types. So, for instance, like, backend types or something might be opaque.

40
00:09:14.620 --> 00:09:17.020
Joseph Denman: As long as you have something… yeah.

41
00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:23.670
Joseph Denman: like, to generalize it, I'd say. I mean, as long as you… have some sort of lightweight.

42
00:09:23.850 --> 00:09:24.370
Joseph Denman: Sure.

43
00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:29.529
Kevin Millikin: They're enumerated, they're enumerated, and so we can have a custom equality for each… for each one.

44
00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:30.750
Kevin Millikin: Kent.

45
00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:39.940
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, we talked about having User-defined opaque types, where you,

46
00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.830
Kent Dybvig: You can provide something like,

47
00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:52.799
Kent Dybvig: a hash function or quality function, you know, there may be a set of things that the object has to satisfy in order to be considered a valid opaque type.

48
00:09:53.630 --> 00:10:04.350
Kent Dybvig: Other than the ones we have built in. So we have… we build in the ones for… for simple types, like the UNT iterate and strings, but then beyond that, it'd have to be serializable, or…

49
00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:11.549
Kent Dybvig: So that we could compare it, or it'd have to be hashable and equal, you know, equable…

50
00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:16.319
Kent Dybvig: It's for the future.

51
00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:25.250
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, just to… to add to that, we… We do have… or… I think the…

52
00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.489
Thomas Kerber: Limitation there is that we need to have a runtime representation of the type.

53
00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:31.879
Thomas Kerber: Or whatever it is.

54
00:10:31.990 --> 00:10:33.969
Thomas Kerber: And our, our runtime.

55
00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:43.229
Thomas Kerber: Requires serialization, and that… once you have serialization, you kind of get the hash equality and the value equality for free.

56
00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:51.439
Thomas Kerber: The challenge we had when we looked into adding user-defined types here is

57
00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.679
Thomas Kerber: More how to let the compiler know of them.

58
00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.319
Thomas Kerber: And it's… it's an API question of…

59
00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:11.940
Thomas Kerber: at the moment, we're just outputting TypeScript, and that would require us to ingest a snippet of TypeScript that defines this type instead, or as well, which is not clear what the best way to do that.

60
00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:20.729
Kent Dybvig: I don't know that we would have to ingest TypeScript. We would just have to know that there was a… there would have to be a method on it.

61
00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:21.630
Kent Dybvig: That we could call.

62
00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:22.330
Thomas Kerber: Yeah.

63
00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:24.370
Kent Dybvig: in the generated TypeScript code.

64
00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:33.599
Thomas Kerber: It's more that there needs to be some TypeScript defining the serialization, deserialization somewhere.

65
00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:39.280
Thomas Kerber: And it's not clear how to link that with the generated TypeScript. You're right, it's not necessary that it would ingest.

66
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:44.009
Kent Dybvig: Well, I think it's just… if it's… if it's just a method call, there's no linking. There's just dynamic linking.

67
00:11:45.570 --> 00:11:46.800
Joseph Denman: But if…

68
00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.229
Thomas Kerber: Not just an effort call, though, because it needs to have the type objects.

69
00:11:51.570 --> 00:12:00.259
Thomas Kerber: available at runtime. It needs… and in particular, if we return this type from Compact, it's not clear where Compact would get those from.

70
00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.089
Thomas Kerber: So this is the…

71
00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:04.410
Kent Dybvig: Okay.

72
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.000
Thomas Kerber: Descriptors in the compiler.

73
00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:07.040
Kent Dybvig: Alright.

74
00:12:07.780 --> 00:12:10.730
Kent Dybvig: This is a good topic for a whiteboard sometime.

75
00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.069
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, exactly.

76
00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:21.759
Kevin Millikin: Yeah, I can imagine that what the compiler generates for a opaque type definition is a descriptor.

77
00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.280
Kevin Millikin: Okay, good.

78
00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:38.460
Kevin Millikin: I'm guessing Kent put the next one on. Should making a cross-contract, or poor Parisa, should making a cross-contract call implicitly disclose the contract address?

79
00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:40.170
Kevin Millikin: and hash…

80
00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.459
Joseph Denman: Of the remote circuit name.

81
00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:55.219
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, this… this comes from, so it's… we have to do a, claim contract call, and claim contract call

82
00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:05.839
Kent Dybvig: Is declared to disclose the address, and it's declared to disclose the hash of the circuit name.

83
00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:09.370
Kent Dybvig: Every single…

84
00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:16.649
Kent Dybvig: cross-contract call will do the same thing. It will disclose the address and hash of the circuit name.

85
00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:23.620
Kent Dybvig: It seems a little mean to require the disclose, rather than just documenting that it's implicitly disclosing.

86
00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:26.600
Kent Dybvig: But if we decide that it should be.

87
00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:43.440
Kent Dybvig: explicitly disclosed by the programmer, then we have a little syntactic issue, which is there's nowhere to put it on the… on the name. Can put it on the address, but we can't put it on… we have no… syntactically, at present, we have no way of attaching a disclose to the name, because the name has to be a symbol.

88
00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:46.320
Kent Dybvig: Syntactically.

89
00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.450
Kent Dybvig: So, my… My vote would be that…

90
00:13:52.570 --> 00:14:00.199
Kent Dybvig: We would leave claim contract call as, something which, isn't implicitly disclosing.

91
00:14:00.490 --> 00:14:06.469
Kent Dybvig: In case somebody has… finds some use for claim contract call, I'm not sure what that would be.

92
00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:19.909
Kent Dybvig: But that we insert discloses when we… when we create the call to claim contract call in… while we translate… when we translate the, cross-confruct call in the compiler.

93
00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:30.780
Thomas Kerber: Kevin, you're mute.

94
00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:33.459
Kevin Millikin: Oh, were you gonna say something, Joe?

95
00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:36.919
Joseph Denman: I'm gonna let Thomas take this in.

96
00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:49.780
Kent Dybvig: Thomas gave the thumbs up, so I think he already took it.

97
00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:54.620
Kevin Millikin: Okay, so what was your… I'm sorry, Ken, what was your proposal exactly, then?

98
00:14:55.460 --> 00:15:01.750
Kent Dybvig: My proposal is that making a cross-contract call implicitly discloses the address

99
00:15:01.890 --> 00:15:07.929
Kent Dybvig: and a hash of the name. And we document that, and we don't require any explicit disclose there.

100
00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:12.690
Kevin Millikin: Like, I mean, I like that as well. Okay.

101
00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:21.249
Parisa Ataei: But, but then this does mean that if someone is calling,

102
00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:29.849
Parisa Ataei: claim contract call, they cannot disclose… they have to disclose the address and the hash of the circuit name.

103
00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:35.560
Parisa Ataei: But syntactically, they still do not have a way to disclose

104
00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:40.090
Parisa Ataei: the circuit name. Whether that's a use case that…

105
00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:47.040
Kent Dybvig: If they're going to, if they're gonna call a claim contract call,

106
00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.460
Kent Dybvig: I mean, first of all, I don't know why they would.

107
00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:52.970
Kent Dybvig: It may… it may even be something we shouldn't expose.

108
00:15:53.710 --> 00:16:01.519
Kent Dybvig: But if they were going to call claim contract call, they're calling it as an ordinary function, and they can… they can wrap discloses around the arguments.

109
00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:05.570
Joseph Denman: It's only because we have this syntax for calls.

110
00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.219
Kent Dybvig: That we have this issue.

111
00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:15.980
Kevin Millikin: Right, they would have to explicitly hash the name to use that API.

112
00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.330
Kevin Millikin: And then they would have to disclose the hash.

113
00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:34.949
Kent Dybvig: All right, sounds like we have no objections. Let's… let's proceed along those lines. I think Bruce has already…

114
00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:36.789
Kent Dybvig: Proceeding that direction.

115
00:16:39.990 --> 00:16:40.850
Kevin Millikin: Sounds good.

116
00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:50.559
Kevin Millikin: And then the last one is, from a practical viewpoint, should we allow a cross-contract call to call a pure circuit?

117
00:16:53.970 --> 00:17:01.110
Parisa Ataei: Right, we had this conversation that whether we want to allow a cross-contract call to call a pure circuit.

118
00:17:01.680 --> 00:17:10.689
Parisa Ataei: But whether we allow it or not, I'm more interested in, is that a practical use case for people to…

119
00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.220
Parisa Ataei: To expect that from cross-contract calls.

120
00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:19.130
Parisa Ataei: And if so, This would just be…

121
00:17:19.329 --> 00:17:24.939
Parisa Ataei: A witness call without any claims, any contract call claims.

122
00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:35.950
Parisa Ataei: And I see that we have, or should it be treated the same as a call to an impure circuit?

123
00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:43.739
Joseph Denman: But check.

124
00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:45.400
Kevin Millikin: Joe?

125
00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:51.849
Joseph Denman: I was just gonna say that, yeah, like, treating it the same as a call to an inverse circuit, where we do the whole…

126
00:17:52.710 --> 00:18:07.160
Joseph Denman: I mean, claim contract call, I don't even know if that would make sense, but if it introduces any additional overhead besides what the original pure circuit would, I would say that we should not do it.

127
00:18:07.470 --> 00:18:16.659
Joseph Denman: Because if, like… I wouldn't want to, like, add an additional proof burden where we don't actually need it.

128
00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:24.469
Joseph Denman: And furthermore, I guess, like, is this even a valid use case? It's a reasonable question, because…

129
00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:28.090
Joseph Denman: I mean, it would…

130
00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:42.379
Joseph Denman: It seems that if… so if both contracts, like the contract… the original contract decliner and the contract user, if they know that this function is going to be shared, then there's always a way to factor out a pure circuit.

131
00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:48.669
Joseph Denman: So the situation that we would need to consider is where… is whether

132
00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:57.960
Joseph Denman: A contract, say, definer or provider does not know that a contract producer wants to use the Cure circuit in the future.

133
00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:06.970
Joseph Denman: Yeah, like, because there's… yeah, there's always a way that you could factor it out, and so you could make the argument that it shouldn't even be, like, in a contract.

134
00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:14.790
Joseph Denman: There is a way to define top-level peer service, right? Like, those are not necessarily defined inside? Yeah, okay.

135
00:19:14.900 --> 00:19:19.379
Joseph Denman: So yeah, I would say that it's not even really a valid use case.

136
00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:24.870
Kevin Millikin: Kent? Kent?

137
00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:39.779
Kent Dybvig: at present, security is, an optional declaration.

138
00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:47.429
Kent Dybvig: And you can call something… you can not… you can omit the peer label on the contract declaration.

139
00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:56.379
Kent Dybvig: And… and treat it as a pure… as an impure circuit.

140
00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:02.650
Kent Dybvig: So, we have to decide… if we're gonna… if we're gonna… outlaw.

141
00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.610
Kent Dybvig: calls to pure circuits.

142
00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:12.160
Kent Dybvig: cross-contract calls to pure circuits, then we would have to, maybe change,

143
00:20:12.950 --> 00:20:15.839
Kent Dybvig: change around how we do things. And…

144
00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:23.720
Kent Dybvig: And I guess the reason I'm feeling uncomfortable with all of this is that I would like for us to keep these contracts at arm's length.

145
00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:32.790
Kent Dybvig: And not understand how their circuits are implemented, and whether even necessarily you know, know whether they're pure.

146
00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:43.550
Kent Dybvig: We might insist they're pure, in which case it'll get checked, and presumably we have to check it at runtime, as well as at compile time, if we have any kind of dynamic code loading.

147
00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:59.800
Kent Dybvig: So, I… I feel uncomfortable with outlying false-to-peer circuits, because we don't know that they are, and… and also for debugging purposes, it made me peer right now because I took out the ledger right.

148
00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:01.380
Kent Dybvig: for testing.

149
00:21:01.780 --> 00:21:04.959
Kent Dybvig: But I still want to test it without the ledger rate.

150
00:21:05.510 --> 00:21:08.380
Kent Dybvig: So, anyway, I'll let somebody else talk.

151
00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:23.779
Kevin Millikin: I guess I'm next. I was… I was just going to flip it around and say that, we should think about the implementation model, so the… what we're proposing that we do, as a first cut of this implementation is that you…

152
00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:31.179
Kevin Millikin: When you make a cross-contract call, you get the binary ZKIR representation of the circuit you're calling from somewhere in the ledger.

153
00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:39.139
Kevin Millikin: And you interpret that binary representation, that ZKII representation, in a little sandbox ZKIR interpreter.

154
00:21:39.740 --> 00:21:42.760
Kevin Millikin: And so pure circuits don't have

155
00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:46.270
Kevin Millikin: such a ZKII representation, so ergo, they can't be called.

156
00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:55.130
Kevin Millikin: And the way that I would actually make this into a language feature is I would say that contracts have an interface, and pure circuits do not appear in the contract interface.

157
00:21:55.340 --> 00:22:02.970
Kevin Millikin: So there's a, a contract declaration implicitly defines maybe a named interface with that same name.

158
00:22:03.020 --> 00:22:15.670
Kevin Millikin: And the pure circuits are not in that interface, so making sure the programmers understand that in some way is kind of… is kind of something for us to start out from an ergonomic standpoint, but that's the way that I would think about it semantically.

159
00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:17.339
Kevin Millikin: And Jay.

160
00:22:20.390 --> 00:22:33.179
Andrzej Kopeć: What is expected to happen, then, if there is a contract interface which is implement… where a circuit appears there and is implemented in a pure fashion?

161
00:22:33.590 --> 00:22:41.420
Andrzej Kopeć: So, like, for example, for the ERC20-like tokens.

162
00:22:41.650 --> 00:22:51.040
Andrzej Kopeć: The interface defines, constants which are returned by actually pure functions.

163
00:22:51.550 --> 00:22:55.810
Andrzej Kopeć: So… So then I would say…

164
00:22:55.810 --> 00:23:01.090
Kevin Millikin: Then I would say that… then I would say that because they appear in the interface, we must generate ZKIR for them.

165
00:23:02.460 --> 00:23:03.840
Kevin Millikin: So that we can interpret them.

166
00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:05.210
Andrzej Kopeć: Makes sense.

167
00:23:11.610 --> 00:23:12.420
Kevin Millikin: Thomas?

168
00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:21.160
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, so I, I think… The whole discussion around purity changes when we have cross-contract calls.

169
00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:29.440
Thomas Kerber: Because it used to be, well, if it's a pure function, there is no point in having this on-chain, because it cannot possibly touch state.

170
00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:34.139
Thomas Kerber: There is point to having pure

171
00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:37.600
Thomas Kerber: Functions on-chain in some cases.

172
00:23:37.880 --> 00:23:40.399
Thomas Kerber: If there's another contract interacting with them.

173
00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:53.009
Thomas Kerber: And I think Andy highlighted one of those, which is, well, you know, if you have a contract wanting to return a constant in there, maybe that constant depends on the address, for example, but you still want it to be dynamically callable.

174
00:23:54.640 --> 00:24:04.430
Thomas Kerber: Like, something else might be… maybe you want to have a contract that just provides something as a library feature, like verifying a certain type of signature.

175
00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:07.270
Thomas Kerber: And that doesn't necessarily interact with state.

176
00:24:07.930 --> 00:24:13.199
Thomas Kerber: So I think we might need to revisit this, this notion of purity a little bit.

177
00:24:13.320 --> 00:24:19.910
Thomas Kerber: I, I don't exactly know… what we do, like, there's this… there's a conflict here, because we still…

178
00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:26.500
Thomas Kerber: don't necessarily want to put all circuits on-chain. But maybe purity is not the right filter for that.

179
00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:37.519
Thomas Kerber: One… one comment I would have is there is a technicality here which might make this tricky and might make it not work, with the ledger directly.

180
00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:41.559
Thomas Kerber: Which is that the ledger expects there is always a transcript.

181
00:24:41.970 --> 00:24:45.109
Thomas Kerber: So even if there is an empty…

